WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
When you build an application for the web,

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.000
the environment feels incredibly smooth and predictable.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.400
You have built-in tools that just work,

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.200
allowing you to move fast and focus entirely on your code.

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.200
That smooth experience is entirely thanks to the web browser.

6
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.200
Think of the browser as a universal translator.

7
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.800
It sits quietly between your React code and the physical device

8
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.400
the user is holding, handling all the complex communication in the background.

9
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.000
If your code needs to save a user's settings,

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.400
you ask for a tool called local storage

11
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.400
and the browser translates that request into instructions for the hard drive.

12
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.400
If you need to find the user on a map,

13
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.000
you call navigator.geolocation

14
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.600
and the browser smoothly fetches the GPS coordinates for you.

15
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.200
Moving to React Native changes this environment completely.

16
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.600
Your React code comes with you, but the browser does not.

17
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.200
You are now working directly on the mobile device

18
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.200
without your trusty translator.

19
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.900
Without that middleman, those familiar requests instantly crash.

20
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.900
The mobile device has no idea what local storage or a navigator object is.

21
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.300
Your code is asking for tools that simply do not exist outside the browser sandbox.

22
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.300
This is exactly what makes the transition

23
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.800
to React Native challenging at first.

24
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.300
You still know exactly how to write React,

25
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.000
but you are temporarily stranded without a built-in way to talk to the phone's hardware.

26
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.200
To fix this broken communication,

27
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.200
the React Native ecosystem provides a solution.

28
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.800
Instead of a single, massive translator,

29
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.600
we use specialized direct connections called native bridges.

30
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.300
These are external libraries,

31
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.300
often from the Expo ecosystem,

32
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.800
that wire your React code directly into the mobile hardware.

33
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.900
Take saving data, for example.

34
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.300
Since we lack a browser's local storage,

35
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.100
we install a bridge library.

36
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.100
On mobile, you will use tools like MMKV or Async Storage

37
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.500
to save data directly to the phone's drive,

38
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.300
which is actually much faster than the web equivalent.

39
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.100
For finding the user on a map,

40
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.200
we replace the web's navigator.geo location

41
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.800
with a library called Expo location.

42
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.400
And if you want to take a photo,

43
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.400
you won't call Get User Media.

44
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.600
You will use a library called Expo Camera,

45
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.300
which gives you a physical component

46
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.500
that you render right inside your React application to display the camera feed.

47
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.200
Fortunately, you do not have to replace everything.

48
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.700
Network requests like fetch or web sockets

49
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.200
do not rely on the browser's document object model to function.

50
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.000
Those specific tools work exactly the same on a mobile phone

51
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.700
as they do on a laptop.

52
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.800
Developing for mobile is largely a phrase book exercise.

53
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.100
You keep your React skills

54
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.100
and simply swap out web specific tools

55
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.400
for mobile specific bridge libraries.

56
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.200
There is a catch to having this direct hardware access.

57
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.400
You now have to negotiate with iOS and Android directly

58
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.700
and mobile operating systems

59
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.000
are incredibly strict gatekeepers when it comes to user privacy.

60
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.300
On the web, asking to use a camera or microphone

61
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.200
is usually a straightforward binary choice.

62
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.200
The user clicks accept or they click deny.

63
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.500
Mobile devices split this decision into five distinct states.

64
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.800
A permission can be granted, denied, blocked, limited or unavailable.

65
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.100
For a developer transitioning from the web,

66
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.700
the most surprising trap on this list is the blocked state.

67
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.300
On a mobile device, if a user permanently denies a permission,

68
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.000
say they tap don't allow on a location prompt,

69
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.400
the operating system locks the door.

70
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.000
Your app is stripped of the ability to ever trigger

71
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.200
that native permission prompt again.

72
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.600
You cannot write a line of code to force that prompt back open.

73
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.800
Instead, you have to build a custom user interface

74
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.400
that politely asks the user to leave your app,

75
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.200
open their phone's central settings,

76
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.200
and manually flip the switch.

77
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.100
Requesting hardware access on mobile

78
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.300
requires a multi-step design strategy

79
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.500
to account for the moments when the operating system

80
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.200
refuses to cooperate.

81
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.200
Knowing how to ask for permissions is step one.

82
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.700
Step two is knowing exactly where to find

83
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.500
the right native bridges for your new features.

84
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.700
Your first stop is the official Expo documentation

85
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.100
at docs.expo.dev.

86
00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.300
If an official Expo library exists, use it.

87
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.500
Next, check the React Native directory

88
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.400
to filter community built libraries by platform and maintenance.

89
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.700
Finally, search the NPM registry for React Native packages.

90
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.000
Following this specific order is a strategic choice.

91
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.900
Libraries from the Expo ecosystem are guaranteed

92
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.700
to be tested against current versions of React Native

93
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.700
and they support rapid development tools

94
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.900
like Expo Go immediately upon installation.

95
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:33.700
Navigating this new ecosystem requires a little patience.

96
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:37.000
Fortunately, you already have the most important skill,

97
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.600
writing interactive logic with React.

98
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.700
Learning this new vocabulary is what allows you to build apps

99
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.900
that use the physical sensors living in your user's pocket.

